## Formatting

### Em-Dash Overuse

**What:** Many em dashes for pauses, asides, and pivots.

**Why it reads as AI:** Humans might use a few per piece; models often use many.

**Avoid:** More than a couple of em dashes per section. Prefer commas, parentheses, or separate sentences.

### Bold-First Bullets

**What:** Every bullet or list item starting with a bolded phrase.

**Why it reads as AI:** Common in model-generated docs and READMEs; rare in hand-written lists.

**Avoid:** Making every bullet "**Keyword**: explanation...". Use bold only when it adds real scan-value.

### Unicode Decoration

**What:** Unicode arrows (→), smart/curly quotes, and other characters that aren't on a standard keyboard.

**Why it reads as AI:** Typists tend to use straight quotes and ASCII arrows (-> or =>).

**Avoid:** "Input → Processing → Output"; "Smart quotes" — use straight quotes and -> or => unless the format requires Unicode.

---

## Composition

### Fractal Summaries

**What:** Summarizing at every level — subsection, section, and document — so the piece repeats the same message.

**Why it reads as AI:** Template-driven structure.

**Avoid:** "In this section, we'll explore... [long passage] ...as we've seen in this section."; long conclusions that restate every point.

### The Dead Metaphor

**What:** One metaphor repeated many times across the piece.

**Why it reads as AI:** Humans tend to use a metaphor once and move on; models repeat it.

**Avoid:** Using the same metaphor (e.g. "ecosystem", "walls and doors", "primitives") 5–10+ times.

### Historical Analogy Stacking

**What:** Rapid-fire listing of companies or tech revolutions to build authority.

**Why it reads as AI:** Especially common in technical writing; feels like filler.

**Avoid:** "Apple didn't build Uber. Facebook didn't build Spotify. Stripe didn't build Shopify..."; "Take Spotify... Or consider Uber... Airbnb followed..."

### One-Point Dilution

**What:** One argument restated in many ways across many words.

**Why it reads as AI:** Padding a simple thesis to feel "comprehensive".

**Avoid:** Turning an 800-word argument into 4000 words of rephrased repetition.

### Content Duplication

**What:** Repeating whole sections or paragraphs verbatim in the same piece.

**Why it reads as AI:** Model losing track of what it already wrote.

**Avoid:** Publishing without a pass to remove duplicate blocks.

### Signposted Conclusions

**What:** Explicitly announcing the end with "In conclusion", "To sum up", or "In summary".

**Why it reads as AI:** Template structure; readers can feel the conclusion without the label.

**Avoid:** "In conclusion, the future of AI depends on..."; "To sum up, we've explored three key themes..."

### "Despite its challenges..."

**What:** Acknowledging problems then immediately dismissing them in a fixed rhythm: "Despite [positive], [subject] faces challenges..." then "Despite these challenges, [optimistic conclusion]."

**Why it reads as AI:** Rigid formula.

**Avoid:** The "Despite X... Despite these challenges, Y" pattern. If you acknowledge problems, address them or leave the tension unresolved.

