## Quick Reference

| Category | Trope / pattern | Prefer / fix |
|---|---|---|
| Clarity | Abstract fog, cheer-words | Actor + action + object you can picture |
| Clarity | Verbal false limbs | One real verb; cut padding phrases |
| Clarity | Needless passive | Active when the agent matters |
| Word choice | Magic adverbs | Plain verbs and nouns; drop intensifiers |
| Word choice | Delve, leverage, utilize | "look at", "use", specific verbs |
| Word choice | Tapestry, landscape | "field", "domain", or be specific |
| Word choice | "Serves as" | "is" / "are" |
| Sentence | "It's not X — it's Y" | Use once per piece at most |
| Sentence | "Not X. Not Y. Just Z." | State the point directly |
| Sentence | "The X? A Y." | Assert; skip the fake question |
| Sentence | Anaphora / tricolon abuse | Vary openings and list length |
| Sentence | "It's worth noting" | Omit or connect to the prior sentence |
| Sentence | False ranges, gerund litany | Real spectra; full sentences |
| Paragraph | Short punchy fragments | Normal sentence rhythm |
| Paragraph | Listicle in prose | Real list or varied openings |
| Tone | "Here's the kicker" | State the point |
| Tone | "Think of it as..." | Explain directly |
| Tone | "Imagine a world..." | Argue without the fantasy opener |
| Tone | Grandiose stakes | Match stakes to the claim |
| Tone | Vague attributions | Name sources or drop them |
| Tone | Invented labels | Standard terms, or define and argue |
| Formatting | Em dashes, bold-first bullets | Fewer dashes; bold only when it helps |
| Formatting | Unicode decoration | Straight quotes, -> or => |
| Composition | Fractal summaries | One summary or none |
| Composition | Dead metaphor, analogy stack | One metaphor; fewer name-drops |
| Composition | Signposted conclusion | End without "In conclusion" |
| Composition | "Despite its challenges" | Address challenges or leave tension |

## Red Flags

- Same sentence structure three or more times in a row
- More than two em dashes in a short passage
- Every bullet starts with a bold label
- "It's not X — it's Y" or "not because X, but because Y" more than once
- "delve", "leverage", "robust", "streamline" in quick succession
- "Imagine", "Think of it as", "Here's the thing" in the first few paragraphs
- "Experts say" or "Studies show" with no citation
- A single metaphor repeated in every section
- "In conclusion" or "To sum up" at the end
- "Despite its challenges" followed by an upbeat ending
- Sentences full of "give rise to", "play a role in", "in order to", "due to the fact that"
- Praise or outcome words with no concrete referent ("unlock value", "seamless", "holistic")
- Passive that hides a known agent ("the bug was introduced", "mistakes were made")

## Before Publishing (Gate)

```
Scan the draft for:
1. Meaning first: Can I picture each claim? Who did what?
2. Orwell checks: long word where a short one works? cuttable words? dead metaphor? needless passive? jargon with an everyday twin?
3. Repeated sentence patterns (anaphora, "It's not X — it's Y", "Not X. Not Y. Just Z.")
4. Overused words from the Avoid lists (delve, leverage, tapestry, landscape, quietly, etc.)
5. Verbal false limbs and abstract fog / cheer-words
6. Filler transitions ("It's worth noting", "Importantly", "Here's the thing")
7. Formatting tics (bold-first bullets, em dashes everywhere, Unicode arrows)
8. Structural repetition (summaries at every level, same metaphor throughout, signposted conclusion)

If several tropes appear, or one appears repeatedly, or the prose stays vague after the tropes are gone, revise for concrete language and variety.
```

A single use of any pattern may be fine. Stacking many tropes, or writing fog without tropes, is the failure mode. Prefer concrete words; let meaning choose them.
